  ],
  "body": [
    {
      "id": "p-intro",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "An "
        },
        {
          "text": "SR latch",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the smallest circuit that can remember a bit. Built from two cross-coupled NAND gates, it is the point where a circuit stops being a pure function of its inputs and starts having state."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-cross",
      "text": "What cross-coupled means",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "p-cross",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Each NAND gate's output is wired back into the other gate's input. That feedback loop is what stores the value: once the outputs settle into a stable pair, they hold it with no further input. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Why this does not oscillate forever",
          "marks": [
            {
              "href": "/tutorials/digital-logic/why-circuits-need-memory",
              "kind": "link"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " is worth reading if the loop bothers you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-active-low",
      "text": "The NAND version is active low",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "p-active-low",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "This is the detail that trips people up. In the NAND version the inputs are usually labeled "
        },
        {
          "text": "S'",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "code"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "R'",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "code"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and they act when pulled "
        },
        {
          "text": "low",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ", not high. Holding both at 1 is the idle state where the latch remembers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-truth",
      "text": "Behavior table",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "t-truth",
      "rows": [
        [
          "1",
          "1",
          "holds",
          "Idle: the latch keeps its previous value"
        ],
        [
          "0",
          "1",
          "1",
          "Set the stored bit to 1"
        ],
        [
          "1",
          "0",
          "0",
          "Reset the stored bit to 0"
        ],
        [
          "0",
          "0",
          "invalid",
          "Both outputs forced to 1, breaking the Q/Q' relationship"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "S'",
        "R'",
        "Q",
        "Meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-forbidden",
      "text": "Why S' = 0, R' = 0 is forbidden",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "p-forbidden",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Pulling both inputs low forces both outputs to 1, which contradicts the invariant that Q and Q' are opposites. Worse, when you release both at once the latch settles into whichever state wins a race, so the result is genuinely unpredictable rather than merely undefined on paper."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-next",
      "text": "Where this leads",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "p-next",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Removing that forbidden combination is what motivates the "
        },
        {
          "text": "gated D latch",
          "marks": [
            {
              "href": "/tutorials/digital-logic/gated-d-latch",
              "kind": "link"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and making it update on a clock edge instead of a level gives you the "
        },
        {
          "text": "D flip-flop",
          "marks": [
            {
              "href": "/tutorials/digital-logic/d-flip-flop",
              "kind": "link"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ", the element real registers are made of."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-build",
      "text": "Build it yourself",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "p-build",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Two NAND gates, cross-wired. Toggle the inputs and watch the output stay put when both return to 1."
        }
      ]
    },
